          ":question/worked-solution": "Computing the triple product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot(b\\times c)&=|\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & -3 \\\\ -1 & k & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 3\\end{aligned}|\\\\ &=((3)\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}k & 0 \\\\ 3 & 3\\end{aligned}|)+((0)\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}|)+((-3)\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}-1 & k \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}|)\\\\ &=((3)\\cdot ((k)\\cdot (3)-(0)\\cdot (3)))+((0)\\cdot ((-1)\\cdot (3)-(0)\\cdot (0)))+((-3)\\cdot ((-1)\\cdot (3)-(k)\\cdot (0)))\\\\ &=((3)\\cdot (3k))+((0)\\cdot (-3))+((-3)\\cdot (-3))\\\\ &=(9k)+(0)+(9)\\\\ &=9k+9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThree vectors are coplanar if and only if their triple product is zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9k+9&=0\\\\ 9k&=-9\\\\ k&=-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the three vectors are coplanar if and only if $k=-1.$",
